Instructor Feedback and Reflection

My instructor’s feedback confirmed that the SQLite persistence enhancement successfully transformed the graphics application from an in-memory 
system into one that can save, retrieve, update, and delete scenes. The enhancement demonstrated my understanding of relational database design, 
normalization, foreign keys, constraints, indexing, prepared statements, transactions, and defensive programming. My explanation also clearly 
connected the database work to Course Outcomes Three, Four, and Five.

One area I can strengthen is my analysis of transaction management and concurrent database access. Although I explained my decision to use SQLite 
with full-mutex mode and a busy timeout, I can expand the discussion to address SQLite’s locking behavior when multiple processes attempt to modify 
the same scene or database. The current design is appropriate for a single-user desktop application, but a future multi-user version might require 
stronger concurrency controls or a client-server database system.

The feedback also recognized my explanation of specific design decisions, including storing the transformation matrix in individual numeric columns 
instead of using a text value or binary blob. Continuing to explain why I selected a particular design—not only what I implemented—will help my 
ePortfolio demonstrate stronger architectural reasoning and engineering judgment.
